---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Sudoku: The Game I Had to Build More Than Once';
const description =
  'How a Python proof of concept, a Unity rebuild, and 52 automated tests changed the way I approached a seemingly simple Sudoku game.';
---

<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Eddie's Games`} description={description}>
  <article class='article'>
    <header class='article-header'>
      <p class='eyebrow'>Behind the Games</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class='dek'>
        Python proved the rules. Unity forced the interface to grow up. Testing
        showed me the difference between playable and reliable.
      </p>
      <p class='meta'>September 17, 2026 · Development Journal</p>
    </header>

    <div class='article-body'>
      <p>Sudoku should have been one of the straightforward ones.</p>

      <p>
        The rules are known, the board is always nine by nine, and the player
        already understands the objective. Compared with inventing an entirely
        new game, it seemed like a sensible project: build the grid, load a
        puzzle, check the answers and call it done.
      </p>

      <p>That was the theory.</p>

      <p>
        In practice, Sudoku became one of those projects that kept teaching me
        something new every time I rebuilt it. The Python version proved that
        the underlying game could work. Rebuilding it in Unity showed how much
        more there was to consider once menus, input, screen sizes and player
        feedback entered the picture. Then automated testing began finding all
        the little assumptions that playing a few rounds had missed.
      </p>

      <p>
        It stopped being one Sudoku project and became a record of how my
        development process was changing.
      </p>

      <h2>Proving the Game in Python</h2>

      <p>
        The first useful version was built in Python. Its job was not to look
        impressive. It was there to prove the mechanics: create a puzzle, accept
        input, protect the original clues and decide whether the completed board
        was correct.
      </p>

      <p>
        That version mattered because earlier attempts at making Sudoku had, to
        use the technical term, flumped out and failed. Sudoku is one of my
        favourite puzzles, but liking a game does not automatically make it easy
        to build. A working Python version finally gave me a reliable foundation
        instead of another half-finished experiment.
      </p>

      <p>
        It also reinforced a lesson I have learned repeatedly: get the game
        working before turning it into a production. A plain but functional
        version answers the important questions. Is the core idea sound? Can the
        game state be represented cleanly? Does the validation behave properly?
        Is it actually enjoyable once it is running?
      </p>

      <p>
        Python answered those questions. The next rebuild created a completely
        different set of them.
      </p>

      <h2>Rebuilding It in Unity</h2>

      <p>
        The Unity version uses Unity 6 and C#, with UI Toolkit handling the
        interface through UXML and USS. It also uses Unity's New Input System,
        because a Sudoku game should not become unusable the moment somebody
        reaches for a keyboard instead of a mouse.
      </p>

      <p>The basic flow is deliberately simple:</p>

      <p class='flow'>
        Main Menu <span>→</span> Gameplay <span>→</span> Game Over
      </p>

      <p>
        The game includes four difficulty settings. Easy starts with 30 visible
        clues, Medium with 22, Hard with 15 and Godlike with only 5. The
        generated answer key remains the authority when validating the player's
        entries.
      </p>

      <p>
        That sounds simple when written in one paragraph. It was less simple
        once the surrounding behaviour arrived.
      </p>

      <p>
        Players needed a notes mode. Given cells had to be protected. Completed
        number buttons needed to become unavailable. Correct and incorrect
        entries required clear visual feedback. The timer had to pause properly,
        and pausing needed to hide the board so that the pause button did not
        become a free thinking-time button. Keyboard input, move counts, light
        and dark themes, completion detection and screen layout all became part
        of the game rather than optional decoration.
      </p>

      <p>
        This was where Sudoku changed from a solved logic problem into a
        user-interface problem.
      </p>

      <p>The rules had not changed at all. Everything around them had.</p>

      <h2>When “It Works” Is Not Enough</h2>

      <p>
        Playing the game is useful testing, but it is also very easy to play the
        way the developer expects the game to be played. Players are far more
        inventive. They click things in the wrong order, enter values you did
        not anticipate and expose combinations of game state that were
        apparently waiting behind a bush with a baseball bat.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Unity project currently has 52 passing EditMode tests. They cover
        puzzle generation and validation, clue counts, cell visibility, game
        state, protection of given cells, number entry, notes and completion
        logic.
      </p>

      <p>
        The number itself is less important than what the tests represent. Each
        one turns an assumption into something the project can check repeatedly.
        If a later change breaks note behaviour or allows a given cell to be
        edited, I should find out immediately rather than after packaging a
        build.
      </p>

      <p>
        Testing also forced some decisions to become explicit. The answer key is
        authoritative. Difficulty settings must produce the expected clue
        counts. Given cells are never editable. Completing the puzzle is a
        specific state transition, not merely a screen that appears when things
        look approximately right.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the point where the test suite became more than a safety net. It
        became part of the design.
      </p>

      <h2>The Interface Still Fights Back</h2>

      <p>
        Automated tests cannot tell me that a Sudoku board is too large for a
        laptop screen or that a control feels awkward on a phone. Those problems
        still require real builds on real screens.
      </p>

      <p>
        UI Toolkit is powerful, but USS changes can absolutely go sideways. A
        small layout experiment can produce an impressively large collection of
        new problems, which led to a useful development rule:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>Commit first. Experiment second.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The WebGL build added another practical lesson: test it through a local
        web server rather than opening <code>index.html</code> directly. A browser
        build is still a web application, and shortcuts that appear to work locally
        can hide problems that show up as soon as the game is served properly.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of these discoveries are glamorous, but they are the work. The
        puzzle generator may be the clever part, yet the player will remember
        the board that did not fit, the button that stopped responding or the
        pause screen that still showed every number.
      </p>

      <h2>What the Rebuilds Changed</h2>

      <p>
        The Python version proved the rules. The Unity version forced me to
        think about the player. The tests forced me to define what “working”
        actually meant.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sudoku is now a playable prototype with the major systems in place, but
        it is not being treated as finished simply because a complete game can
        be played. Layout testing, interface polish, platform testing and
        release-style packaging still matter.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rebuilding the same idea was not wasted effort. Each version answered
        questions the previous version could not. More importantly, each one
        changed how I approached the next project.
      </p>

      <p>
        That may be the real history of this Sudoku game: not a straight line
        from idea to finished product, but a series of increasingly better
        questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class='article-footer'>
      <a href='/articles/'>← All articles</a>
      <a href='/'>Play the games →</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

